1. Backend Deployment to AWS EC2
Purpose

This workflow:

Builds the Node.js backend application
Creates a deployment package
Uploads the package to an EC2 instance
Deploys the application
Restarts the application using PM2
Sends deployment status notifications via email
EC2 Server Requirements

The target EC2 instance must have the following installed:

Node.js
node -v
NPM
npm -v
PM2
pm2 -v

Install PM2 if not available:

npm install -g pm2
TAR Utility
tar --version
Application Directory

Create the deployment directory:

sudo mkdir -p /var/www/myapp
sudo chown -R ubuntu:ubuntu /var/www/myapp

Replace ubuntu with your EC2 username if different.

SSH Access

GitHub Actions connects to the EC2 server through SSH.

Verify SSH access:

ssh -i private-key.pem ubuntu@your-server-ip

Connection should work successfully before using the workflow.

Backend Project Requirements

The project should contain:

{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "...",
    "test": "..."
  }
}

Expected build output:

dist/
└── server.js

PM2 starts the application using:

pm2 start dist/server.js --name myapp
Required GitHub Secrets

Navigate to:

Repository
→ Settings
→ Secrets and Variables
→ Actions

Create the following secrets:

Secret Name	Description
EC2_HOST	EC2 Public IP or DNS
EC2_USER	SSH Username
EC2_SSH_KEY	Private SSH Key
SMTP_USERNAME	Email Sender Address
SMTP_PASSWORD	SMTP Password / App Password
EMAIL_TO	Recipient Email Address(es)
Example
EC2_HOST=54.xxx.xxx.xxx
EC2_USER=ubuntu
EMAIL_TO=devops@company.com

2. Email Notification Configuration
Purpose

Email notifications are sent when a deployment:

Succeeds
Fails
Gets cancelled

Notifications include:

Repository information
Branch information
Deployment result
Commit details
Workflow execution link

GitHub Secrets

Store:

SMTP_USERNAME=your-email@gmail.com
SMTP_PASSWORD=<generated-app-password>
EMAIL_TO=team@company.com

Note: Do not use your normal Gmail password.